WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.420
- Thanks to CuriosityStream

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:02.740
for keeping Legal Eagle in the air,

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:04.400
which now comes with Nebula for free.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:06.290
Link in the description.

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:07.620
The Supreme Court's decisions

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:10.230
on President Trump's tax returns are in.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.460
Now, remember there is
no legal requirement

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:15.170
that presidential candidates
or presidents for that matter

00:00:15.170 --> 00:00:18.010
release their tax returns
from any particular year.

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:20.240
But despite the lack of legal requirement

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.210
for the last 30 years,

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:23.780
almost all presidential and
vice presidential candidates

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.410
have released years of
their personal tax returns.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:27.243
And during that time,

00:00:27.243 --> 00:00:28.970
every person nominated for the president

00:00:28.970 --> 00:00:30.420
as a Democrat or Republican

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.670
has released their tax information,

00:00:32.670 --> 00:00:34.990
except for President Donald Trump.

00:00:34.990 --> 00:00:36.380
The self-proclaimed billionaire

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.640
whose companies filed
for bankruptcy six times

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:40.550
has been extremely guarded about releasing

00:00:40.550 --> 00:00:42.710
any information about
his financial status.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:44.820
Well, he said he would release them.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:49.310
- I will release my tax returns
against my lawyer's wishes.

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:51.720
I will absolutely give my return.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.140
- But then decided not to.

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:54.670
- Well, I'm not releasing tax returns,

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:56.320
'cause, as you know, they're under audit.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:58.550
- The financial information
supposedly revealed

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:00.560
that President Trump was deeply in debt

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.710
for much of the 1980s and 1990s,

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:05.590
and that he borrowed lots of
money from overseas entities,

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:09.010
and that in multiple years he
claimed more financial losses

00:01:09.010 --> 00:01:12.120
than all but two other U.S. taxpayers.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.050
Some people reacted to this
news with the phrase "So what?"

00:01:15.050 --> 00:01:17.200
- You know, the only one that
cares about my tax returns

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.280
are the reporters, okay?

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:19.113
They're the only ones.

00:01:19.113 --> 00:01:20.370
- While others believe
that the revelations

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:22.370
were just the tip of the iceberg.

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:24.210
But in 2018, the New York Times said

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.440
that they obtained years of financial data

00:01:26.440 --> 00:01:29.130
about President Trump and
his various organizations

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:30.310
from anonymous sources,

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:33.370
which we now know include the
president's niece, Mary Trump,

00:01:33.370 --> 00:01:37.090
who is writing her own
biography of the president.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:37.990
But the state of New York

00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:40.240
subpoenaed President
Trump's financial records

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:41.770
in connection with potentially

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.080
the campaign finance
violations that Michael Cohen,

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.920
the president's lawyer, has
already gone to jail over.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:48.120
Meanwhile, in Congress,

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.550
three oversight committees of
the House of Representatives

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.750
has subpoenaed President
Trump's accounting firm

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:55.100
and multiple banks looking for information

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.250
about the president's financial history.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:59.740
President Trump challenged
all of these subpoenas,

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:02.460
and the cases wound up
in the Supreme Court.

00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:03.293
Although the cases

00:02:03.293 --> 00:02:05.410
obviously include
President Trump directly,

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:07.060
the stakes go far beyond that

00:02:07.060 --> 00:02:09.320
in the upcoming presidential election.

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:10.830
These decisions will determine

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:13.240
just how much power the
president, any president,

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:14.500
not just the sitting president,

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:16.800
has to control the legal process

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:18.940
against the presidency in the future.

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:20.890
And these cases are a mixed bag.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.733
Everyone wins and everyone loses.

00:02:23.733 --> 00:02:26.566
(news reel music)

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:30.500
Hey, Legal Eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:33.440
because it looks like some of
the president's information

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:35.890
is going to be disclosed
to district attorneys

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:37.490
and potentially the public.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:40.020
The Supreme Court has just
decided two major cases

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:41.180
regarding the accessibility

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:42.850
of a sitting president's tax information

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:44.290
and financial records.

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:45.460
And what did they decide?

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.410
And what does this mean
for President Trump

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:49.430
and his upcoming bid for reelection?

00:02:49.430 --> 00:02:51.910
Well, let's dive in and
get down to brass tacks.

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:54.750
This is a tale of two cases.

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.060
The very long 2020 Supreme Court term

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:58.810
has finally come to a close

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:00.510
with the Court finally resolving

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:02.660
the two cases involving Donald Trump's tax

00:03:02.660 --> 00:03:03.820
and financial information.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:04.930
So how did we get here?

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:07.920
Well, the president claims to
be a self-made billionaire.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:11.280
- My father gave me a
very small loan in 1975,

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:12.500
and I built it into a company

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.490
that's worth many, many
billions of dollars.

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.930
- So why is it so taxing
for President Trump

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:18.750
to show his taxes?

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:21.190
Well, we don't know what, if anything,

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:23.890
the president is trying to
hide in breaking with precedent

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:26.550
and refusing to disclose his tax returns,

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:28.440
but that picture might
have been made clearer

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:29.630
when the New York Times published

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:31.787
a Pulitzer Prize-winning article entitled

00:03:31.787 --> 00:03:34.007
"Trump Engaged in Suspect Tax Schemes

00:03:34.007 --> 00:03:36.330
"as He Reaped Riches From His Father."

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.690
The article was a deep dive

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:39.340
into the finances of Donald Trump

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:41.320
and his father, Fred Trump Sr.,

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.870
as well as the rest of
his immediate family.

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:45.890
The article made two major findings.

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:47.190
First, President Trump

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:49.540
got a whole lot more
than a $1 million loan

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:50.790
from his father, Fred Trump,

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:53.010
as the president had always claimed.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:55.540
Secondly, President Trump
along with his siblings

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:57.580
engaged in a variety of tax schemes

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:00.640
that many tax experts said were illegal.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.070
It's a fascinating read

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:04.070
that definitely deserves
to be readdressed,

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:06.420
especially since we've sort of moved on

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.170
from this particular scandal.

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:09.800
But some of the highlights include

00:04:09.800 --> 00:04:12.900
that not only did
President Trump dissemble

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.770
when he claimed that he only
received a $1 million loan

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:18.000
from his father to start his own business,

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:19.707
which he allegedly paid
back with interest,

00:04:19.707 --> 00:04:21.000
The New York times discovered

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.840
that actually President
Trump's father loaned him north

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:28.080
of $60 million at the time or
$140 million in today's money.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:32.500
And that he received at least
$413 million in today's money

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:33.950
from his father's estate,

00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:36.220
much of which might have
been received by dubious

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:38.060
and potentially illegal tax schemes

00:04:38.060 --> 00:04:40.710
he engaged in along with his siblings.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.170
And these schemes included

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:44.220
potentially creating sham corporations

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.980
to disguise gifts from
President Trump's parents

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:49.370
to himself and his his siblings

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:52.480
to just undervaluing
several real estate holdings

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:55.040
in their parents' estate
to avoid paying more taxes

00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:56.710
on those particular holdings.

00:04:56.710 --> 00:04:59.560
And some in the Trump family
have already faced consequences

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:00.970
from The New York Times article.

00:05:00.970 --> 00:05:03.560
For example, President Trump's
sister, Maryanne Trump Barry,

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:06.100
once a federal judge, was forced to retire

00:05:06.100 --> 00:05:08.240
after the article disclosed
that she and her siblings

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:10.050
were co-owners of a shell company

00:05:10.050 --> 00:05:12.950
that allegedly siphoned cash
from their father's business

00:05:12.950 --> 00:05:15.030
by just making up purchases.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:16.910
In addition, the inflated receipts

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:19.050
were allegedly used to
justify rent increases

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:20.570
for thousands of tenants.

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:23.480
Barry quit in order to stop a
judicial ethics investigation

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:24.810
into her conduct, though,

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.700
she did get to keep her $200,000 pension.

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:29.690
On the other hand, the
president has managed

00:05:29.690 --> 00:05:32.630
to escape scrutiny of his own taxes,

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:36.910
largely by stonewalling the
House and district attorneys

00:05:36.910 --> 00:05:39.920
who are asking for that
financial information.

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:42.110
And, as a result, at this time the public

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:44.760
can't be certain that the
president is either guilty

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:47.300
or innocent of these allegations,

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:49.260
which takes us to the
two Supreme Court cases

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:50.680
that were just decided.

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:53.500
The first is captioned
as Trump versus Vance.

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:55.410
Vance in this case being the famous,

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:57.070
some would say infamous,

00:05:57.070 --> 00:05:59.050
New York district Attorney Cy Vance.

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:01.050
Now I know it seems like ancient history,

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:02.060
but you might recall

00:06:02.060 --> 00:06:04.230
that the president's old
lawyer, Michael Cohen,

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:05.780
went to prison for tax evasion

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:07.800
and violating campaign finance law.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.800
His crimes were paying an adult
film star, Stormy Daniels,

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.020
and former Playboy model, Karen McDougal,

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:15.380
to stay quiet about their
alleged sexual encounters

00:06:15.380 --> 00:06:16.810
with President Trump.

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:19.100
Now there's no way to know for sure,

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:21.430
but it's possible that President Trump

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:24.840
was the unindicted
co-conspirator individual one

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:28.050
who engaged in a conspiracy
with Michael Cohen.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.060
Of course, the name has been redacted,

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.180
so it's impossible to know for certain

00:06:32.180 --> 00:06:35.620
that President Trump was in
fact individual number one.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:38.220
But not withstanding, the
district attorney for New York,

00:06:38.220 --> 00:06:41.760
Cyrus Vance, subpoenaed Trump's
accounting firm Mazars USA

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:44.300
for eight years of business
and personal tax records

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:46.130
in connection with President Trump

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:48.410
and the Trump organization's
potential role

00:06:48.410 --> 00:06:50.940
in the hush money payments
made to McDougal and Daniels

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:52.840
before the 2016 election.

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:54.770
This is a criminal investigation

00:06:54.770 --> 00:06:56.480
into President Trump's possible role

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:58.870
in these violations of
law, since Cohen claimed

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:01.280
to be acting on President Trump's behalf.

00:07:01.280 --> 00:07:04.110
- I am ashamed that I chose to take part

00:07:04.110 --> 00:07:07.190
in concealing Mr. Trump's illicit acts

00:07:07.190 --> 00:07:09.720
rather than listening
to my own conscience.

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:11.970
- The president filed for
emergency injunctive relief,

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:14.180
claiming that as president
the constitution provides him

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:15.240
with absolute immunity

00:07:15.240 --> 00:07:17.730
from any criminal
process or investigation,

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:20.240
including unofficial presidential conduct

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:23.070
and the associated subpoenas
of third party records.

00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:25.100
This means that President
Trump thinks he has a right

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:28.070
to stop Mazars and the
banks Vance also subpoenaed

00:07:28.070 --> 00:07:29.750
from complying with the subpoenas.

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:31.520
And as a result of the injunction,

00:07:31.520 --> 00:07:33.790
those financial records
were never turned over

00:07:33.790 --> 00:07:35.160
to the state of New York.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:37.920
So let's talk about President
Trump's arguments here.

00:07:37.920 --> 00:07:40.170
President Trump argued
that now is not the time

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:42.480
to bring any potential
criminal charges against him.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:44.420
In the president's view,
there are only two options

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:46.010
for dealing with criminal allegations

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:47.430
against a current president.

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:49.650
One, Congress can impeach the president,

00:07:49.650 --> 00:07:51.260
which we've crossed that bridge,

00:07:51.260 --> 00:07:53.500
or a state can launch a
criminal investigation

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:56.880
after he or she ends
their term as president.

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:59.840
The president contended that
defending a criminal inquiry

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:01.270
would interfere with his ability

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:03.230
to discharge his duties as president,

00:08:03.230 --> 00:08:04.920
and he asserted that criminal charges

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:06.760
are actually prohibited
by the Supremacy Clause

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:08.300
of Article II of the Constitution

00:08:08.300 --> 00:08:09.610
since state and local officials

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:11.577
are simply using their authority to

00:08:11.577 --> 00:08:13.817
"defeat the legitimate operations

00:08:13.817 --> 00:08:15.180
"of the federal government."

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:17.730
The president argued that it's
not necessarily this one case

00:08:17.730 --> 00:08:19.620
that would distract him
from presidential duties.

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:22.347
Rather it is "the cumulative effect

00:08:22.347 --> 00:08:24.767
"of permitting every
state and local prosecutor

00:08:24.767 --> 00:08:27.610
"to take the same steps the
district attorney did here."

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:29.560
And while the president
was making it sound like

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:31.410
there are thousands of local prosecutors

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:33.260
waiting to prosecute the president,

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:35.150
really he was arguing
sort of the same thing

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:37.620
that Bill Clinton once did
in Clinton versus Jones

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:41.230
that any action taken against
him would also ipso facto

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:45.310
be politically motivated and
would swamp the presidency

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:48.480
in responding to all of
these different inquiries.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:50.050
Some of which might be legitimate,

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:52.600
but the vast majority of
which might not be legitimate

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:55.940
and might be filed by
Democratic district attorneys

00:08:55.940 --> 00:08:57.400
to stymie a Republican president

00:08:57.400 --> 00:08:59.110
or Republican district attorneys

00:08:59.110 --> 00:09:01.300
to stymie a Democratic president.

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:03.500
In response, District
Attorney Vance simply said

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:04.660
that there's no such thing

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:06.430
as unlimited presidential immunity

00:09:06.430 --> 00:09:07.830
from criminal prosecution.

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:09.190
Vance argued persuasively

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:10.710
that the president's Article II immunity

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:14.350
only extends to official acts
in his capacity as president.

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:16.760
And prior precedent
involving sitting presidents

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:19.620
like Clinton versus Jones
and Nixon versus Fitzgerald

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:21.300
bolstered Vance's arguments.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:23.930
When Paula Jones sued Bill
Clinton for sexual harassment

00:09:23.930 --> 00:09:25.780
that happened before he was president,

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:27.650
the Court ruled that he was not entitled

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:29.540
to absolute immunity from civil litigation

00:09:29.540 --> 00:09:30.890
arising out of events

00:09:30.890 --> 00:09:33.640
which transpired prior
to his taking office.

00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:35.480
And that lawsuit was not dismissed,

00:09:35.480 --> 00:09:37.640
and Clinton had to comply
with the legal process.

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:39.730
Eventually he sat for a deposition,

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:41.870
which he famously perjured himself.

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:42.810
And on the other hand,

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:45.300
the Supreme Court has ruled in
the past that President Nixon

00:09:45.300 --> 00:09:47.830
did have absolute immunity from damages

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:50.960
predicated on his official
acts as president.

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:51.793
Things that happened

00:09:51.793 --> 00:09:54.600
while he was the president
of the United States.

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:56.780
But even in that case, the
Supreme Court emphasized

00:09:56.780 --> 00:09:59.460
that a president is not
immune from criminal charges

00:09:59.460 --> 00:10:01.310
stemming from his official

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:03.720
or unofficial acts while in office.

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:05.080
And here Vance argued

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:07.130
the same limitation on
presidential immunity

00:10:07.130 --> 00:10:08.840
also extends to the Supremacy Clause,

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:10.780
which supposedly prevented states

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:13.290
from interfering in the
president's official acts.

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:15.540
Vance argued that state
criminal investigations

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:16.810
and subsequent subpoenas

00:10:16.810 --> 00:10:19.550
are both unrelated to the
president's official acts

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:20.620
and would not interfere

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:22.250
with President Trump's official duties

00:10:22.250 --> 00:10:23.820
as president of the United States.

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:25.610
And Vance also pointed out

00:10:25.610 --> 00:10:27.560
that allowing a president
to have total immunity

00:10:27.560 --> 00:10:29.390
from criminal or civil litigation

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:31.860
during the time the president is in office

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:33.350
could lead to total immunity

00:10:33.350 --> 00:10:35.570
after one's presidential term has ended,

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:37.680
as well as immunity from third parties.

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:39.690
Because if that immunity existed,

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:42.330
it might impede the criminal investigation

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:44.030
because you might lose the evidence

00:10:44.030 --> 00:10:46.470
or the statute of limitations might run

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:49.120
because too much time has passed.

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:51.960
So let's talk about how
the Supreme Court ruled

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:53.410
in this particular case.

00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:57.260
Here, Vance won and Trump
lost, at least on paper.

00:10:57.260 --> 00:10:59.480
The Supreme court ruled
seven justices to two

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:00.987
that in our judicial system

00:11:00.987 --> 00:11:03.770
"the public has a right
to every man's evidence."

00:11:03.770 --> 00:11:05.280
In practice, this means that the president

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:06.870
does not have absolute immunity

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:08.420
from state criminal subpoenas.

00:11:08.420 --> 00:11:11.200
And state prosecutors do not
need to meet a higher standard

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:13.370
to seek evidence from a sitting president.

00:11:13.370 --> 00:11:14.590
Although President Trump argued

00:11:14.590 --> 00:11:17.110
that criminal cases would
distract him from his job,

00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:19.407
Justice Roberts disagreed stating,

00:11:19.407 --> 00:11:21.237
"two centuries of experience confirm

00:11:21.237 --> 00:11:23.577
"that a properly tailored
criminal subpoena

00:11:23.577 --> 00:11:25.357
"will not normally hamper the performance

00:11:25.357 --> 00:11:27.280
"of the President's
constitutional duties."

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:28.920
And one of the things that
led to people thinking

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:31.220
that this was a complete
slam dunk of a case

00:11:31.220 --> 00:11:32.370
is that these subpoenas

00:11:32.370 --> 00:11:34.280
weren't directed at the president himself,

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:36.570
but rather third parties,
accountants and banks.

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:38.670
So it's not like the president
was going to have to spend

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:40.980
all of his time responding
to the subpoenas anyway.

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:42.910
These records are held by third parties,

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:45.370
and President Trump cannot
tell those third parties

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:46.770
that they can't respond.

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:47.733
And although President Trump argued

00:11:47.733 --> 00:11:49.110
that criminal investigations

00:11:49.110 --> 00:11:51.140
would harm the public perception of him,

00:11:51.140 --> 00:11:53.140
the majority opinion
also dismissed this claim

00:11:53.140 --> 00:11:55.480
by noting that the issuance of a subpoena

00:11:55.480 --> 00:11:56.870
would not have much of an effect

00:11:56.870 --> 00:11:58.520
on the president's reputation.

00:11:58.520 --> 00:11:59.710
Because when you get a subpoena,

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:01.090
you have to provide the information.

00:12:01.090 --> 00:12:04.230
There's no necessary stigma
related to that in particular.

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:05.750
Moreover, grand jury proceedings,

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:07.480
like what was going on in New York,

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:10.610
are strictly confidential,
which, as the court pointed out,

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:12.030
should prevent the very stigma

00:12:12.030 --> 00:12:13.780
that the president anticipates.

00:12:13.780 --> 00:12:16.260
Chief Justice Roberts, who
wrote the opinion of the Court,

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:17.970
also dismissed the president's concern

00:12:17.970 --> 00:12:19.669
about thousands of local prosecutors

00:12:19.669 --> 00:12:21.430
trying to make a name for themselves

00:12:21.430 --> 00:12:23.280
by investigating the president.

00:12:23.280 --> 00:12:24.870
The court pointed out
that the legal system

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:26.740
already has safeguards to protect against

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:30.300
the abuse of prosecutors
that the president envisions.

00:12:30.300 --> 00:12:32.150
And while it's true that
the Supremacy Clause

00:12:32.150 --> 00:12:33.170
bars local officials

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:35.200
from interfering with
the president's duties,

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:37.480
if a prosecutor is attempting
to usurp the president

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:39.610
rather than simply investigate a crime,

00:12:39.610 --> 00:12:41.510
then the president can
challenge that in court,

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:43.920
which is exactly what President
Trump did in this case.

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:45.730
And while the president and
the Department of Justice

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:47.600
argued that state prosecutors

00:12:47.600 --> 00:12:49.270
should be subject to a higher standard

00:12:49.270 --> 00:12:51.020
in order to get evidence
from the president,

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:52.250
the Supreme Court disagreed

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:54.130
holding that it would not be proper

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:55.780
for state criminal subpoenas

00:12:55.780 --> 00:12:58.220
to be subject to a higher standard.

00:12:58.220 --> 00:12:59.817
And the broader point was that,

00:12:59.817 --> 00:13:02.867
"the public interest in fair
and effective law enforcement

00:13:02.867 --> 00:13:06.090
"cuts in favor of comprehensive
access to evidence."

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:09.210
So if state grand juries had
to meet a higher standard,

00:13:09.210 --> 00:13:11.727
it would, "hobble the grand jury's ability

00:13:11.727 --> 00:13:14.157
"to acquire all information
that might possibly bear

00:13:14.157 --> 00:13:16.290
"on its investigation."

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:18.320
The Court also echoed Vance's concern

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:20.580
that the evidence would be
lost if prosecutors had to wait

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:22.460
until the end of the
president's term to see it.

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:25.110
At the end in this first
case, Trump versus Vance,

00:13:25.110 --> 00:13:26.350
the Court remanded the case

00:13:26.350 --> 00:13:29.020
back to the lower court of
appeals for additional argument.

00:13:29.020 --> 00:13:30.870
In that argument President Trump

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:33.180
can make new arguments about the subpoenas

00:13:33.180 --> 00:13:35.530
and try to prevent the
subpoenas from being enforced.

00:13:35.530 --> 00:13:37.440
But he can't argue that he is immune

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:39.540
or that a higher standard should apply.

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:41.884
While there are still
legal questions to decide,

00:13:41.884 --> 00:13:44.390
President Trump is probably
going to lose most,

00:13:44.390 --> 00:13:46.960
if not all of the battles that
he is going to have to fight

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:48.630
in the court of appeals.

00:13:48.630 --> 00:13:52.230
So while the biggest battle
is over, the war is not.

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:55.580
And President Trump will probably
continue to make arguments

00:13:55.580 --> 00:13:58.170
that these specific
subpoenas are overbroad

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:00.330
or make other smaller arguments

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:02.240
about why they shouldn't be enforced.

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:04.370
So it's possible that this
issue will not be resolved

00:14:04.370 --> 00:14:05.810
before the 2020 election.

00:14:05.810 --> 00:14:08.330
Though, since this was a
grand jury proceeding anyway,

00:14:08.330 --> 00:14:11.100
it wasn't going to be open
to the public in any event.

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:12.980
Which takes us to the other tax case

00:14:12.980 --> 00:14:15.880
that was decided at the same
time, Trump versus Mazars.

00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:19.000
While Trump versus Vance was
a defeat for the president,

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:22.000
Trump versus Mazars some argue
that the president prevailed,

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:24.480
at least because he's
going to delay the effect

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:26.280
of the decision, at least for now.

00:14:26.280 --> 00:14:28.110
This case was kicked
off when President Trump

00:14:28.110 --> 00:14:30.570
sought injunctive relief on four subpoenas

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:32.970
issued by three separate house committees:

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:34.700
the House Financial Services Committee,

00:14:34.700 --> 00:14:36.180
the House Intelligence Committee,

00:14:36.180 --> 00:14:37.950
and the House Oversight Committee.

00:14:37.950 --> 00:14:40.900
These subpoenas were sent to
Trump's accounting firm again,

00:14:40.900 --> 00:14:43.350
as well as to financial
institutions he used

00:14:43.350 --> 00:14:45.240
for personal and business reasons.

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:46.460
The goal of these subpoenas

00:14:46.460 --> 00:14:47.770
according to the House committees

00:14:47.770 --> 00:14:49.880
is to exercise their
basic roles of oversight

00:14:49.880 --> 00:14:51.490
in legislative responsibilities.

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:53.910
Now, how would this information be used?

00:14:53.910 --> 00:14:55.770
Well, the committee said
it would inform Congress

00:14:55.770 --> 00:14:57.060
on subjects like whether they need

00:14:57.060 --> 00:14:59.840
to impose new international
money laundering restrictions

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:02.190
to evaluate current ethics
laws for presidents,

00:15:02.190 --> 00:15:04.370
or determine if it should be mandatory

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:07.150
to have future presidents
disclose their tax returns.

00:15:07.150 --> 00:15:09.220
And obviously it goes without saying

00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:13.130
that it might inform past and
future impeachment inquiries.

00:15:13.130 --> 00:15:14.550
Now, Donald Trump argued

00:15:14.550 --> 00:15:16.560
that congressional committees
are only authorized

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:19.170
to issue subpoenas for a
true legislative agenda,

00:15:19.170 --> 00:15:21.220
not for any law enforcement reason.

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:22.540
That would be the purview

00:15:22.540 --> 00:15:24.150
of the executive branch exclusively.

00:15:24.150 --> 00:15:26.280
And President Trump also
made the very broad claim

00:15:26.280 --> 00:15:28.027
of executive privilege stating that,

00:15:28.027 --> 00:15:29.617
"Whatever powers Congress holds,

00:15:29.617 --> 00:15:31.957
"it may not deploy them in a
way that keeps the President

00:15:31.957 --> 00:15:34.510
"from fulfilling the
obligations of his office."

00:15:34.510 --> 00:15:36.120
Now the House of Representatives argued

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:37.970
that this matter had already been decided

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:40.460
citing Clinton versus Jones
in which a sitting president

00:15:40.460 --> 00:15:42.320
was not immune from civil litigation.

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:43.687
The House committees argued that,

00:15:43.687 --> 00:15:45.667
"Article II does not
confer on a president,

00:15:45.667 --> 00:15:48.560
"the power to resist compulsory
process directed at him,"

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:51.897
unless "there is impairment
of the Executive's ability

00:15:51.897 --> 00:15:55.140
"to perform its constitutionally
mandated functions."

00:15:55.140 --> 00:15:56.150
The House committees argued

00:15:56.150 --> 00:15:58.400
that this is clearly not
the case in this situation

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:01.760
since the subpoenas were not
even directed at the president.

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:04.260
So there's no actual burden
on the president himself.

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:06.660
Congress argued that it
has the legislative purpose

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:09.530
for seeking information if
a subpoena seeks information

00:16:09.530 --> 00:16:11.040
that will inform Congress on a subject

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:13.000
that would likely lead to legislation.

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:15.860
Well, detractors argued that
these assertions were so broad

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:16.820
that it would allow the House

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:19.090
to seek personal
information from a president

00:16:19.090 --> 00:16:23.280
about anything if it somehow
could be used to justify a law.

00:16:23.280 --> 00:16:24.740
The Supreme Court ruled seven to two

00:16:24.740 --> 00:16:27.370
in a way that sort of splits
things down the middle.

00:16:27.370 --> 00:16:29.360
The Court did reject
President Trump's claim

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:32.150
that he can raise executive
privilege over the information

00:16:32.150 --> 00:16:34.890
and did acknowledge that
Congress has the power

00:16:34.890 --> 00:16:36.970
to subpoena information from the president

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:39.240
so it can craft legislation effectively.

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:41.300
However, the Supreme
Court limited that power

00:16:41.300 --> 00:16:43.190
and held that congressional
subpoenas are valid

00:16:43.190 --> 00:16:46.230
only they serve a "valid
legislative purpose"

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:49.550
and are not intended to be
law enforcement efforts.

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:51.380
And the Supreme Court for the first time

00:16:51.380 --> 00:16:53.740
articulated a four-part test

00:16:53.740 --> 00:16:56.840
to determine if the
subpoenas can be enforced

00:16:56.840 --> 00:16:58.300
against the executive branch.

00:16:58.300 --> 00:17:00.400
But just as it did in the Vance case,

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:02.850
the Supreme Court rejected
President Trump's argument

00:17:02.850 --> 00:17:05.780
that Congress needs to prove
that the records are critical

00:17:05.780 --> 00:17:08.020
or some incredibly high standard.

00:17:08.020 --> 00:17:10.970
Applying such a high standard
here Robert's explained,

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:13.497
again, he wrote the opinion
for the Supreme Court,

00:17:13.497 --> 00:17:15.617
"would risk seriously impeding Congress

00:17:15.617 --> 00:17:17.800
"in carrying out its responsibilities."

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:20.100
But the Court also dismissed
arguments by the House

00:17:20.100 --> 00:17:21.730
that these subpoenas
should be treated like

00:17:21.730 --> 00:17:23.700
any other request for documents.

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:25.937
The Court held that the House
did not take into account

00:17:25.937 --> 00:17:28.497
"the significant
separation of powers issues

00:17:28.497 --> 00:17:30.367
"raised by congressional subpoenas

00:17:30.367 --> 00:17:32.180
"for the president's information,"

00:17:32.180 --> 00:17:34.140
and articulated that when lower courts

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:36.750
have to decide whether to
enforce a congressional subpoena

00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:39.257
into records involving the
president, the courts should

00:17:39.257 --> 00:17:41.867
"perform a careful analysis
that takes adequate account

00:17:41.867 --> 00:17:44.040
"of the separation of
powers principles at stake."

00:17:44.040 --> 00:17:45.670
Among other things, courts should consider

00:17:45.670 --> 00:17:48.200
whether the president's
papers are really necessary,

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:50.620
because the information
cannot be obtained elsewhere,

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:53.650
whether the subpoena is as
limited in scope as it can be

00:17:53.650 --> 00:17:55.720
while still serving Congress's power,

00:17:55.720 --> 00:17:58.050
what evidence Congress
has offered to establish

00:17:58.050 --> 00:18:01.420
that a subpoena advances a
valid legislative purpose,

00:18:01.420 --> 00:18:04.420
and what burdens a subpoena
imposes on the president.

00:18:04.420 --> 00:18:05.620
And because the lower courts

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:07.490
did not adequately consider these factors,

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:08.850
probably because these factors

00:18:08.850 --> 00:18:11.040
didn't exist before
the Supreme Court case,

00:18:11.040 --> 00:18:12.920
the cases will now return to those courts

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:14.640
for additional proceedings.

00:18:14.640 --> 00:18:16.880
So there were two crucial questions posed

00:18:16.880 --> 00:18:19.010
by the Vance and Mazars cases.

00:18:19.010 --> 00:18:20.450
First, can of state subpoena

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:22.240
a current president's personal records

00:18:22.240 --> 00:18:24.160
as part of a criminal investigation?

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:25.850
And the answer to that is yes,

00:18:25.850 --> 00:18:27.260
a state prosecutor has the right

00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:28.710
to subpoena a sitting president

00:18:28.710 --> 00:18:30.450
or third parties who hold information

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:31.830
about the current president.

00:18:31.830 --> 00:18:33.720
And those inquiries are not bound

00:18:33.720 --> 00:18:35.550
by any kind of higher burden

00:18:35.550 --> 00:18:39.020
than would normally happen
under a state investigation.

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:40.730
And the second question
was whether Congress

00:18:40.730 --> 00:18:42.670
can subpoena a president's
personal information

00:18:42.670 --> 00:18:45.060
outside of a formal impeachment inquiry?

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:47.410
And the answer is basically yes,

00:18:47.410 --> 00:18:49.580
but although Congress may have the right

00:18:49.580 --> 00:18:51.800
to subpoena personal
information about the president,

00:18:51.800 --> 00:18:53.460
it will need to show that there is really

00:18:53.460 --> 00:18:55.500
a legislative purpose for the information,

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:58.250
and Congress can't necessarily
do a fishing expedition

00:18:58.250 --> 00:19:00.170
into the president's personal information.

00:19:00.170 --> 00:19:02.070
In the end, the Court
affirmed that the president

00:19:02.070 --> 00:19:03.680
is not above the law,

00:19:03.680 --> 00:19:05.360
but the justices also kick the can

00:19:05.360 --> 00:19:07.980
down the road a little longer,
pretty much guaranteeing

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:10.300
that we won't see the
president's financial records

00:19:10.300 --> 00:19:12.550
before the vote in November.

00:19:12.550 --> 00:19:13.710
Now I have a lot more to say

00:19:13.710 --> 00:19:15.940
about the political
implications of these decisions

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:17.050
and what it means in terms

00:19:17.050 --> 00:19:19.750
of the public actually getting
access to the tax returns,

00:19:19.750 --> 00:19:22.210
but that's probably not safe for YouTube,

00:19:22.210 --> 00:19:25.010
which is why I put my
extended discussion on Nebula.

00:19:25.010 --> 00:19:27.790
My creator friends and I teamed
up to build our own platform

00:19:27.790 --> 00:19:29.290
where creators don't need to worry

00:19:29.290 --> 00:19:31.810
about demonetization or
the dreaded algorithm.

00:19:31.810 --> 00:19:32.750
It's called Nebula,

00:19:32.750 --> 00:19:35.410
and we're thrilled to be
partnering with CuriosityStream,

00:19:35.410 --> 00:19:36.590
because Nebula is a place

00:19:36.590 --> 00:19:39.070
where creators can do
what they do best, create.

00:19:39.070 --> 00:19:41.860
It's a place where we can
both house our content ad-free

00:19:41.860 --> 00:19:43.850
and also experiment with original content

00:19:43.850 --> 00:19:46.370
and a new series that probably
wouldn't work on YouTube.

00:19:46.370 --> 00:19:48.300
In fact, if you liked this episode,

00:19:48.300 --> 00:19:51.240
the version that I put up on
Nebula removes this ad entirely

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:53.300
and replaces it with
that extended discussion

00:19:53.300 --> 00:19:55.280
about the president's tax returns

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:57.130
and all of the implications there.

00:19:57.130 --> 00:19:59.320
The bar has been set so low

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:03.800
that rejecting these
incredibly stupid arguments

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:04.990
is considered a win.

00:20:04.990 --> 00:20:06.710
Nebula features lots of YouTube's

00:20:06.710 --> 00:20:09.660
top educationalist
creators, like Thomas Frank,

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:11.690
Knowing Better, Polyphonic,
and Lindsay Ellis,

00:20:11.690 --> 00:20:12.740
and tons of others.

00:20:12.740 --> 00:20:14.000
We also get to collaborate in ways

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:15.190
that wouldn't work on YouTube,

00:20:15.190 --> 00:20:17.680
like Tom Scott's amazing
game show "Money,"

00:20:17.680 --> 00:20:20.060
where he pits a bunch of famous
YouTubers against each other

00:20:20.060 --> 00:20:22.370
in psychological experiments
where they can work together

00:20:22.370 --> 00:20:23.830
or profit individually.

00:20:23.830 --> 00:20:25.960
It is amazing, you will love it.

00:20:25.960 --> 00:20:28.330
So what does this have to
do with CuriosityStream?

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:29.490
Well, as the go-to source

00:20:29.490 --> 00:20:31.550
for the best documentaries
on the internet,

00:20:31.550 --> 00:20:34.370
they love educational content
and educational creators.

00:20:34.370 --> 00:20:35.250
And we worked out a deal

00:20:35.250 --> 00:20:36.810
where if you sign up for CuriosityStream

00:20:36.810 --> 00:20:38.230
with the link in the description,

00:20:38.230 --> 00:20:40.950
not only will you get
access to CuriosityStream,

00:20:40.950 --> 00:20:43.550
but you'll also get a Nebula
